{} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "13 – 15 August 1936"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/320"} "Schwimmstadion, Reichssportfeld, Berlin"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/5098"} "20 from 10 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Fourteen (14) year old " [:a {:href "/athletes/46268"} "Ragnhild Hveger"] " had set the first of her 42 world records early in 1936 and, although that record was for the 800 metres, she had emerged as a strong contender for this Olympic title. This was confirmed by her heat swim, in which she broke the Olympic record. Waiting for Hveger in the final, however, was the " [:a {:href "/results/5087"} "100 metre"] " champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/49366"} "Rie Mastenbroek"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hveger pushed out to a lead in the final and tried to shake off the presence of the Dutch swimmer, but it was to no avail, and Mastenbroek sprinted ahead with less than ten metres left to win her third gold of the Games. Mastenbroek later told of an incident that happened during before one of the earlier races of the Games. Hveger was handing out chocolates to some of the other swimmers, but not to Mastenbroek. This angered the Dutchwoman, who was determined to get her “revenge” in the 400m race."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Hveger would go on to dominate the European Championships in London in 1938, and set world bests at both freestyle and backstroke before her retirement. Coming out of retirement to compete in the Helsinki Olympics, she was still good enough at the age of 32 to reach the final of the " [:a {:href "/results/5337"} "400 metre freestyle"] ". Judging by the times she recorded, it is possible that, if she competed between 1940 and 1948, her medal tally may have been in double figures."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [